  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In early 2022, the Damascus Chamber of Industry (DCI) adopted a strategic plan for 2022-23. In March 2021, DCI commenced a process to assess its internal capacity. This led to the decision to develop a new strategic plan for the years 2022-23 as it had been operating without a strategic plan since 2013. In early 2022, DCI embarked on a strategic planning exercise by consulting the board, conducting a members&#x2019; needs assessment, and engaging secretariat staff. The Board endorsed the new strategic plan in March 2022 and reviewed by the new Board in Feb 2023. In line with the plan, DCI improved its value proposition to members by enhancing its networking and service offer. Additionally, the Chamber implemented a multi-channel communication strategy with regular postings on Telegram and social media. DCI also transformed its website to include a portal with members&#x2019; access only for critical information and advisory services; revamped website contents to include infographics and FAQs; and established a dedicated line for labour-market queries. DCI invested resources to improve its research capacity (one staff hired) and expanded the services of its labour department by delivering advice and training on labour law and social security law compliance. Between Nov 2022 and March 2023, DCI organized 8 training events attended by 100 companies. Tapping into its enhanced research capacity, DCI conducted a skills needs assessment in Adra Industrial Zone with aims to advocate for the need to re-open TVET centres within the Industrial Zone.
Following a market assessment study on OSH services as envisioned in the strategic plan, DCI identified a gap in the market and decided to offer advice and information on OSH to members by hiring a dedicated staff on a part-time basis. 
As a result of adopting and implementing its strategic plan, DCI institutionalized its labour-related capacity, enhancing value proposition and growth of the organization. The ILO assisted the DCI to assess its internal organizational capacity, to facilitate inclusive consultation process with Board members and to develop proposals to operationalize the findings of the members&#x2019; needs assessment. These building blocks provided the core elements of the strategic plan drafted by DCI with ILO support.
Additionally, the ILO supported the implementation of selected areas of the adopted strategic plan, including through the following:
1. Introducing capacity development activities for the staff in DCI&#x2019;s labour department to develop new material (PPTs, FAQs, infographics, short videos) on how to comply with labour law and social security law;
2. Providing guidance on conducting a market study assessing the demand for training and advisory services in the area of Occupational Safety and Health;
3. Technically guiding the research study on skills needs anticipation in Adra industrial zone and conducting a feasibility study to re-operationalize the Training Centre in Adra Industrial Zone.</narrative>
   </description>
